Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 17:42:10 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] ks_body: move never-run function bodies to the code store

A block that never ran has no frame and no instantiated nested function, so its body and
everything nested in it are referenced only from its own constants. At the idle round,
each never-run child of a block that ran leaves its body to the store, one record per
block of its subtree, and keeps a shell (flags, length, name, toString span, bytecode)
that function objects hold. The first read of the body fetches its record (checked by
hash), rebuilds it, and makes each nested block a shell over its own record, so loading
is per level, as lazy compilation would be. A subtree's scopes travel as one table in its
root record, rebuilt under the anchor: the first scope up the chain that a block which
ran holds. Nested blocks' bytecode store copies pass to the records and then to the
shells rebuilt from them, so each is released once. A subtree that nests too deep, mixes
scripts, or would hang from two scopes stays resident (bodySkips).
